**This test fails on any host that is not macOS.**

```ts
it('reads the setting the project gives', () => {
  expect(workerSandboxOn(repoWith('workerSandbox: true\n'))).toBe(true);
  expect(workerSandboxOn(repoWith('workerSandbox: false\n'))).toBe(false);
});
```

This PR also adds a throw to `workerSandboxOn` for when the setting is on and the platform is not `darwin` (`src/core/project-config.config.ts` L68-76):

```ts
if (on && process.platform !== 'darwin') {
  throw new Error(`... ${WORKER_SANDBOX_KEY} is refused outside macOS ...`);
```

Nothing fakes the platform for line 60:
- `vitest.config.ts` has no setup files.
- The file has no `beforeEach`.
- Only the `it.each` table at L76-92 overrides `process.platform`, and it restores the real value afterwards.

On Linux, line 60 therefore throws. pup supports Linux hosts (`PUP_SANDBOX=vm`; `src/core/sandbox.utils.ts` L388-390 covers pup gating itself on Linux), so pup's test stage goes red there. It passes on the author's macOS gate, which is why it went unseen. The rest of the suite keeps platform-dependent assertions host-independent with `onPlatform(...)` helpers.

Gating the test loses no coverage: the `{ platform: 'darwin', refused: false }` row at L77 already asserts `true` with darwin faked, and L91 already asserts `false` on every platform.

